      "ballot_id": "B025",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Charles L. Kane",
        "Eugene J. Mele",
        "Laurens W. Molenkamp"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The quantum spin Hall effect and topological insulators established a new class of matter whose robust boundary states follow from bulk topology, a deep and influential advance in condensed-matter physics.",
        "attribution": "Kane and Mele developed the decisive theoretical framework; Molenkamp's experiments demonstrated the quantum spin Hall state in HgTe quantum wells.",
        "maturity": "The basic topological classification and experimental signatures are well established, with many material realizations now known.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The listed discovery groups together the original two-dimensional effect and the broader topological-insulator concept, which developed through a wider community."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B039",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Antoine Georges",
        "Dieter Vollhardt",
        "Gabriel Kotliar"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Dynamical mean-field theory made a powerful, nonperturbative treatment of local electronic correlations practical and reshaped the quantitative study of strongly correlated materials.",
        "attribution": "Georges, Kotliar, and Vollhardt were central to establishing the framework and its applications; the proposed trio is credible, although the method grew from a wider theory community.",
        "maturity": "The method has been extensively developed, benchmarked, and used alongside experiment for decades.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The award case is for a theoretical method rather than one sharply bounded discovery, and credit could extend to other early contributors."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B030",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Jagadeesh S. Moodera",
        "Stuart S. P. Parkin",
        "Terunobu Miyazaki"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Room-temperature tunnel magnetoresistance enabled practical magnetic readout and spintronic devices, connecting a clear physical effect to lasting technological impact.",
        "attribution": "Moodera, Parkin, and Miyazaki are associated with pivotal demonstrations of large room-temperature effects in magnetic tunnel junctions; the contribution of earlier work and parallel groups needs careful delineation.",
        "maturity": "The effect is firmly established and deployed in technology, with its underlying spin-dependent tunneling physics well understood.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The candidate combines the room-temperature breakthrough with giant tunnel magnetoresistance, whose experimental milestones and priority claims are not identical."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B005",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Immanuel Bloch",
        "Markus Greiner",
        "Peter Zoller"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical-lattice quantum simulators turned clean, controllable many-body systems into laboratories for strongly correlated physics, including landmark demonstrations of the Mott-insulator transition.",
        "attribution": "Bloch and Greiner led foundational experiments; Zoller supplied central theoretical proposals for quantum simulation with cold atoms.",
        "maturity": "The platform is mature, reproducible, and productive, although its reach as a quantitatively reliable simulator for difficult material problems is still developing.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The candidate groups a broad program of theory, platform development, and later demonstrations that involved many researchers."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
